                <h2>Case Introduction: A Ramadan Creative Flop</h2>
<p>A utility app team urgently launched Indonesia-targeted ads two weeks before Ramadan, featuring a "Limited Time Offer" with red lantern backgrounds and "Hot Sale" copy. Expected ROI: 1.5. Actual CTR: 0.3%, with CPM 40% above market average. After investigation, the team discovered the issue: Indonesian users during Ramadan dislike promotional inducements, and "Hot" carries negative connotations in Muslim contexts.</p>
<p>This wasn't a quality problem—it was a localization mindset failure.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 1: Language Localization ≠ Text Translation</h2>
<p><strong>Problem Identification</strong>: The team outsourced localization to a translation agency. Delivered copy was grammatically correct but contextually off.</p>
<p><strong>Actions Taken</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Build a localization review checklist covering religious holidays, homophones, and political sensitivities</li>
<li>Prioritize hiring Muslim-background reviewers for Indonesian content</li>
<li>For non-Latin scripts (Arabic, Thai, Vietnamese), use native voiceovers over subtitles</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Result</strong>: After refreshing creatives in the second week of Ramadan, CPM dropped 28% and CTR recovered to 1.1%.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Recommended localization budget allocation: Cultural adaptation 40%, Linguistic accuracy 30%, Visual style 30%.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Mistake 2: Ignoring LATAM Payment Gaps</h2>
<p><strong>Problem Identification</strong>: A cross-border e-commerce brand ran Dynamic Ads in Mexico and Brazil with only PayPal and credit card payment options. After three months, cart abandonment stayed above 72%. Investigation revealed that 65%+ of Brazilian users prefer Boleto Bancário and PIX, while Mexican users favor OXXO cash payments.</p>
<p><strong>Actions Taken</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>Integrate PIX, Boleto, and local credit card installments on Brazil sites</li>
<li>Integrate OXXO and SPEI transfers on Mexico sites</li>
<li>Segment ad audiences by payment preference at the country level</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Result</strong>: Brazil conversion rate improved from 0.8% to 2.3%; Mexico improved to 1.9%.</p>
